from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('6');add=A.add
add('2.4-01','A force can deform a body or contribute to a change in its velocity.','For constant mass, the resultant force determines acceleration through F = ma; zero resultant allows rest or constant velocity.',
'A force is an interaction between objects, measured in newtons. Newton’s first law states that an object keeps its velocity unless a resultant force changes it. A nonzero driving force can be balanced by resistance, so the presence of a force does not alone establish acceleration. Newton’s second law relates acceleration to the vector sum of the forces, not to one selected force when others also act. A force can also change size or shape when a body deforms.',
'Explain why a constant-mass object accelerates when the forces on it have a nonzero resultant.','The forces do not balance, so their vector sum is nonzero.~By F = ma, the acceleration is in the direction of that resultant.')
add('2.4-02','A resultant force can change speed, direction or both.','A change of velocity can occur even when the speed itself stays unchanged.',
'A ball speeding up along a straight line changes velocity through its magnitude. A ball slowing down has a velocity change opposing its motion. A moving object that turns also changes velocity because direction is part of a vector. In each case identify the resultant force rather than assuming that the direction of motion is the direction of the force. A force can act on a moving body without increasing its speed.',
'State one way a resultant force can change motion besides increasing the speed.','It can change the direction of motion.')
add('2.4-03','A force description should identify its type, the object exerting it and the object receiving it.','Several different interactions can act on the same body at the same time.',
'A book on a table experiences gravitational attraction from Earth and a contact force from the table. These forces act on the book, but come from different agents. A rope can add a tension, and sliding can introduce friction. A force diagram should show the forces on the chosen body, not a mixture of forces on several unrelated objects. Labelling the interaction clarifies which forces must be combined when finding that body’s resultant.',
'Explain the link between a force agent and a force diagram for a book resting on a table.','Earth exerts the book’s weight and the table exerts its contact force.~Both arrows belong on a diagram of forces on the book because the book is the receiving body.')
forces=[('M01','A normal contact force acts perpendicular to the contact surface.','Its direction follows the surface orientation rather than a fixed upward direction.','For a book on a horizontal desk the support force is upward, but a wall’s normal force is horizontal. Other contact components, such as friction, can act along the surface. Identify the relevant contact before choosing an arrow direction. A support force need not equal weight if other forces or vertical acceleration affect the body.','State the direction of a normal contact force relative to its surface.','Perpendicular to the contact surface.'),('M02','Electrical forces arise from interactions involving electric charge.','They can attract or repel depending on the charge arrangement.','A charged rod can exert a force without touching another charged object. In simple charge comparisons, like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Attraction can also occur through induced charge redistribution, so attraction by itself does not establish that an initially neutral object carried the opposite net charge. Distinguish electrical interactions from the magnetic action of a magnet.','State the type of force responsible for repulsion between two similarly charged objects.','An electrical or electrostatic force.'),('M03','Magnetic forces act in interactions involving magnets, magnetic materials or suitable moving charges and currents.','Two magnetic poles can attract or repel according to their orientation.','A bar magnet can pull an iron object without direct contact. Two magnets can repel, whereas attraction alone does not prove that a piece of iron is itself a permanent magnet. Magnetic force direction should be inferred from the actual pole or field arrangement. A magnet does not attract every material simply because it is a solid.','State the force involved when a magnet attracts an iron nail.','A magnetic force.'),('M04','Weight is the gravitational force on a body.','Near Earth, its direction is toward Earth’s centre rather than away from the supporting surface.', 'A suspended load still has weight even when a rope prevents it falling. Its mass is a property measured in kilograms, whereas weight is a force measured in newtons. A surface or rope may balance that weight, but balance does not remove the gravitational interaction. The mass–weight calculation is developed in topic 7.','State the force exerted by Earth on a falling stone.','Its weight, or gravitational force.'),('M05','Tension is the pulling force transmitted by a taut rope, string or cable.','At the attachment it acts along the rope away from the object being pulled.', 'A simple rope can pull a load but does not push it along its length in the same way. Draw the tension direction from the actual rope segment attached to the chosen body. A slack rope may transmit no appreciable tension. Do not automatically set tension equal to weight when the load accelerates or when the rope is inclined.','State the direction of tension on a load attached to a taut vertical rope above it.','Upward along the rope, away from the load.'),('M06','Friction opposes relative sliding or its tendency at a contact; air resistance opposes relative motion through air.','These resistive forces are distinct from the weight of the object.', 'A sliding block can slow because of surface friction, while a falling object can experience upward air resistance. The size depends on the interaction and conditions. Friction is not necessarily backward relative to the overall motion of a vehicle: it can provide the forward force from a driven tyre. Specify the bodies or medium when identifying which relative motion is opposed.','State the force that resists a parachutist’s downward motion relative to the air.','Air resistance, or drag.'),('M07','The force agent is the body or field source exerting the interaction on the chosen object.','Naming the receiving object prevents forces on different bodies from being mixed in one resultant.', 'For a magnet pulling a steel pin, the magnet is the agent and the pin receives the described force. For a hand pulling a rope, identify whether the question concerns the rope or an attached load before labelling its force. A complete statement such as the rope pulls the load upward communicates more than the word tension by itself.','State the force agent when a table supports a book through contact.','The table is the agent exerting the contact force on the book.')]
for key,*values in forces:add('2.4-03-'+key,*values)
add('2.4-04','Collinear forces can be combined using signed components along a chosen positive direction.','Opposing directions use opposite signs, so the resultant may be smaller than either of the competing force totals.',
'With east positive, a pull of +18 N and resistance of −7 N give a resultant of +11 N. If the values become +7 N and −18 N, the resultant is −11 N, meaning west. Do not add magnitudes when arrows oppose. A zero resultant can occur with several nonzero forces acting. Combine forces on the same body when applying the result to its acceleration.',
'Calculate the resultant of a 24 N eastward force and a 9 N westward force.','Take east as positive: resultant = 24 − 9.~The resultant is 15 N east.')
add('2.4-05','Spring extension is loaded length minus unloaded length.','Within a proportional range, extension is directly proportional to the applied load.',
'Measure the unloaded spring length before adding loads. At each known load, record the new length and subtract the original length. Plot extension against force to see whether the trend is a straight line through the origin. If force is vertical and extension horizontal, the slope is stiffness k; reversing these axes gives reciprocal stiffness. Increase loads gently, avoid overloading and let the spring settle before reading. The loaded length itself is not the extension unless the original length were zero.',
'Draw an extension-versus-load graph for forces 0, 1, 2, 3 N producing extensions 0, 4, 8, 12 mm.','Plot force horizontally and extension vertically with units.~Draw the straight trend through the origin; its gradient is 4 mm/N.')
add('2.4-06','Within its limit of proportionality, a spring obeys F = kx, where x is extension and k is stiffness.','In SI units F is in newtons, x in metres and k in newtons per metre.',
'If 3 N extends a spring by 0.015 m within its proportional range, k is 200 N/m. Use extension rather than total length and convert millimetres to metres before obtaining k in N/m. A larger k means more force is required for the same extension. Do not assume the relation remains valid after the extension-load trend curves away from proportionality. The graph orientation determines whether the measured slope is k or its reciprocal.',
'Calculate the force needed for an extension of 0.020 m when k = 150 N/m and the spring remains in its proportional range.','F = kx = 150 × 0.020.~The required force is 3.0 N.')
add('2.4-07','The limit of proportionality marks where force and extension cease to follow a proportional straight-line relationship.','Beyond that point, F/x is not represented by one unchanged proportional stiffness.',
'Look for departure from the initial straight trend in an extension-load graph. This limit is not automatically the same as the point at which the spring breaks or remains permanently stretched; those are different observations. If the data extend beyond the proportional region, do not use a single earlier k value to predict the entire response. In practical work use a suitable load range and stop before damage or unsafe loads occur.',
'State how the limit of proportionality is identified on an extension-load graph.','It is where the trend first departs from the initial proportional straight line.')
add('2.4-08','For constant mass, resultant force equals mass multiplied by acceleration.','The acceleration direction is the direction of the resultant force, expressed using a consistent sign convention.',
'A 2 kg trolley with a resultant force of 6 N has acceleration 3 m/s². If 6 N is only the driving force and a resistance also acts, first calculate the resultant before using F = ma. Convert grams to kilograms when using newtons and m/s². A body can move steadily with a nonzero driving force if another force balances it. Its present velocity is not itself a measurement of acceleration.',
'Calculate the acceleration of a 3.0 kg trolley acted on by a resultant force of 12 N east.','a = F/m = 12/3.0.~The acceleration is 4.0 m/s² east.')
add('2.4-09','Momentum is mass multiplied by velocity.','It is a vector, so its direction or signed component follows the velocity direction.',
'A 0.50 kg object moving west at 4 m/s has momentum 2 kg m/s west. With east chosen positive, the component is −2 kg m/s. A stationary object has zero momentum even though its mass is not zero. When several objects are considered, add signed momentum components rather than adding speed magnitudes indiscriminately. The unit kg m/s is equivalent to N s, linking momentum change with a force acting over time.',
'Calculate the momentum of a 0.80 kg trolley moving at 2.5 m/s east.','p = mv = 0.80 × 2.5.~The momentum is 2.0 kg m/s east.')
add('2.4-10','A resultant force acting over a time interval changes momentum.','For a constant or appropriate average resultant force, Δp = FΔt.',
'If a 10 N average resultant acts for 0.20 s, the momentum change is 2.0 N s in the force direction. The change is final momentum minus initial momentum. If an object stops, its final momentum is zero and the change opposes its initial motion. For the same required momentum change, increasing the stopping time reduces the average force. That is why cushioning can reduce a stopping force without changing the original momentum that must be removed.',
'Calculate the momentum change caused by an average resultant force of 15 N acting east for 0.40 s.','Δp = FΔt = 15 × 0.40.~The momentum change is 6.0 kg m/s east.')
add('2.4-11','A system’s total momentum is conserved when no external resultant force acts on that system.','Momentum can transfer between its interacting bodies while the system total stays unchanged under that external-force condition.',
'Choose the system boundary before applying the principle. Forces between two colliding trolleys are internal to the combined two-trolley system, but a push from a person outside it is external. Individual trolley momenta need not stay constant during their interaction. If the external resultant is negligible during a short collision, comparing the total immediately before and after is a useful model. Do not extend the conclusion to a system receiving a significant external impulse.',
'Define the principle of conservation of momentum, including its condition.','The total momentum of a system remains unchanged when its external resultant force is zero.')
add('2.4-12','In a one-dimensional collision model with no external resultant force, total signed momentum before equals total signed momentum after.','For objects that remain together, their shared final momentum is their combined mass multiplied by their common velocity.',
'Two trolleys of masses 0.30 kg and 0.20 kg initially moving at +2 m/s and zero have total momentum +0.60 kg m/s. If they remain together and the external resultant is negligible, their common velocity is +1.2 m/s. Opposite initial directions require opposite signs. For a rebound, retain the final velocity sign as well. The supplied masses and directions, rather than a memorised all-positive formula, determine each term. Only the one-dimensional momentum application is needed here.',
'Two trolleys of 0.40 kg at +3.0 m/s and 0.20 kg at rest remain together after collision. Calculate their common velocity if the external resultant force is negligible.','With negligible external resultant force, momentum gives (0.40 × 3.0) = (0.40 + 0.20)v.~The common velocity is +2.0 m/s.')
A.misconception('MIS-04','Constant velocity means zero acceleration and therefore zero resultant force for constant mass.','Individual forces can still be present and balanced.',
'A powered cart can travel steadily while a motor drives it forward and resistance acts backward. If the forward force is 14 N and the motion has constant velocity, the opposing total is also 14 N in the simple collinear model. The resultant is zero, not 14 N. Removing the drive while resistance remains changes the balance and can slow the cart. The error is confusing a named applied force with the total force on the body.',
'A cart moves at constant velocity with a 14 N forward driving force. Explain why its resultant is not 14 N.','Its constant velocity gives zero acceleration, so the resultant is zero.~Other forces provide a 14 N opposing total that balances the drive.',guidance=['Identify the confusion: a driving force is not the resultant (1 mark).','Correct explanation: opposing forces balance, giving zero acceleration and zero resultant (1 mark).'])
A.misconception('MIS-07','Total momentum conservation requires no external resultant force on the chosen system.','An external impulse can change the momentum of that system.',
'Compare two coasting trolleys interacting with each other and a trolley being pushed by a person outside the selected system. Internal transfers can preserve the combined trolley momentum when the external resultant is negligible. The outside push supplies a momentum change, so the same unchanged-total statement is not justified. Naming the system and its external forces matters more than simply recognising that a collision or movement occurs.',
'A person gives an external push to a single trolley system. Explain why one cannot simply assume its momentum stays constant during that push.','Momentum is conserved for the selected system only when its external resultant force is zero.~The outside push can supply an external impulse and change the trolley’s momentum.',guidance=['Identify the confusion: the external-force condition for momentum conservation is missing (1 mark).','Explain the correct alternative: an external impulse changes the system momentum (1 mark).'])
A.misconception('MIS-08','Opposing velocities use opposite signs in a one-dimensional momentum sum.','With no external resultant force, that signed sum is the quantity conserved through the interaction.',
'Choose east as positive. A 2 kg object moving east at 3 m/s contributes +6 kg m/s, while a 1 kg object moving west at 2 m/s contributes −2 kg m/s. The total is +4 kg m/s, not the sum of the speed magnitudes. If the objects remain together with negligible external resultant, divide that signed total by the combined mass. A negative answer in another example would mean common motion west under the same convention.',
'East is positive. A 2 kg trolley moves east at 3 m/s and a 1 kg trolley west at 2 m/s. Explain the error in adding their momenta as 6 + 2.','The westward velocity contributes −2 kg m/s, not +2.~The correct signed total is +4 kg m/s; this total is conserved if the external resultant force is zero.',guidance=['Identify the direction-sign error (1 mark).','Correct total: +4 kg m/s, with the external-force condition stated if applying conservation (1 mark).'])
A.plot('2.4-05',[0,1,2,3],{'Extension':[0,4,8,12]},'Force / N','Extension / mm','Proportional extension, inverse-stiffness slope')
C=A.choice
C('2.4-03-M01','What is the direction of a normal contact force?',('Perpendicular to the contact surface','The normal direction is defined by the surface.'),('Along the surface','That can describe friction instead.'),('Geographically north','A surface normal is not a compass direction.'),('Along the object’s velocity','Normal contact direction is not set by velocity.'))
C('2.4-03-M05','Which interaction is tension?',('A taut string pulling a load','The string transmits a pull along itself.'),('Earth attracting a stone','That is gravitational force.'),('Air slowing a falling object','That is drag.'),('A table pressing perpendicular to a book','That is normal contact.'))
C('2.4-01','A cart moves with constant velocity. What is its resultant force in a constant-mass model?',('Zero','Zero acceleration implies zero resultant.'),('Equal to its forward drive regardless of resistance','The other forces must be included.'),('Its mass multiplied by its speed','That gives momentum, not force.'),('Necessarily its weight downward','Other vertical forces can balance weight.'))
C('2.4-05','What is spring extension?',('Loaded length minus unloaded length','It is the increase in length.'),('Loaded length alone','This includes the original length.'),('The original length divided by the load','This has a different meaning and unit.'),('The load multiplied by total length','This is not a length change.'))
C('2.4-07','Where does the limit of proportionality occur?',('Where the initial proportional graph trend ends','The force/extension ratio ceases to be constant.'),('Where the spring necessarily breaks','Breaking is a different event.'),('At the first nonzero load for every spring','The initial proportional region can extend over many loads.'),('At zero extension only','The zero point does not define the end of the proportional range.'))
C('2.4-11','What condition permits a system’s total momentum to be conserved?',('No external resultant force','This excludes a net external momentum transfer.'),('Every body has zero velocity','A moving system can conserve momentum.'),('Every body has equal mass','Equal masses are not required.'),('No internal interactions occur','Internal interactions can transfer momentum between bodies.'))
C('2.4-09','Which unit represents momentum?',('kg m/s','Momentum is mass times velocity.'),('kg m/s²','This is a force unit.'),('N/m','This can represent spring stiffness.'),('J/s','This is power.'))
C('2.4-04','Forces of 17 N east and 5 N west act on one body. What is their resultant?',('12 N east','The opposing components subtract.'),('22 N east','This adds opposing magnitudes.'),('12 N west','This reverses the stronger direction.'),('0 N','The forces are unequal.'))
C('2.4-06','A spring within its proportional range has k = 80 N/m and extension 0.025 m. What is the force?',('2.0 N','Use F = kx.'),('3200 N','This divides instead of multiplying.'),('80.025 N','This adds quantities with different units.'),('0.0003125 N','This reverses the ratio.'))
C('2.4-08','A 4 kg object has a 10 N resultant force. What is its acceleration?',('2.5 m/s²','Divide resultant force by mass.'),('40 m/s²','This multiplies force and mass.'),('0.4 m/s²','This reverses the quotient.'),('10 m/s²','This ignores the stated mass.'))
C('2.4-09','A 0.30 kg trolley moves at 4 m/s east. What is its momentum?',('1.2 kg m/s east','Multiply mass by velocity and retain direction.'),('13.3 kg m/s east','This divides velocity by mass.'),('0.075 kg m/s east','This divides mass by velocity.'),('1.2 kg m/s west','This reverses the momentum direction.'))
C('2.4-10','An average resultant of 8 N acts east for 0.25 s. What momentum change does it cause?',('2 N s east','Multiply force by its duration.'),('32 N s east','This divides by time.'),('8.25 N s east','This adds incompatible quantities.'),('0 N s','A nonzero external resultant over time changes momentum.'))
P=A.part
P('2.4-03-M05','State the name of the pulling force in a taut rope.','Tension.',1,'One mark for tension.')
P('2.4-07','State the graph feature indicating the end of spring proportionality.','The trend departs from its initial proportional straight line.',1,'One mark for the departure from the proportional line.')
P('2.4-01','Explain why balanced forces can act on a cart moving steadily along a straight track.','Steady velocity means zero acceleration and zero resultant; a forward drive can be matched by the opposing resistance.',2,'One mark for the zero-acceleration condition; one for matching opposing forces.')
P('2.4-06','Calculate k for a spring extended by 0.030 m under 6 N within its proportional range.','k = F/x = 6/0.030 = 200 N/m.',2,'One mark for force divided by extension; one for 200 N/m.')
P('2.4-08','Calculate the acceleration of a 2.5 kg body under a resultant force of 15 N west.','a = F/m = 15/2.5 = 6.0 m/s² west.',2,'One mark for the relation and substitution; one for the correctly directed result.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Use force horizontally and extension vertically. Plot the six idealised readings and a straight trend through the origin within the stated proportional range.',unit='mm/N',meaning='The plotted gradient is extension per force, so it is reciprocal stiffness in these units; k must instead be obtained as force per extension.',method=['Secure a spring vertically and place a ruler alongside it with a clear pointer or reference on the extending part.','Measure and record the unloaded length before applying any load.','Apply a known calibrated force gently and read the loaded length after oscillations settle.','Subtract the original length to obtain extension and repeat the reading where appropriate.','Repeat for several safe forces, keeping the spring within its proportional range.'],control='Use the same spring and reference point, read the scale perpendicularly and protect against falling loads or overload.',table='Record force / N, original length / mm, loaded length / mm and extension / mm, with repeated readings kept separately.',conclusion='Plot extension against force to test the proportional trend. Read its slope as extension per force; use the reciprocal with consistent unit conversion if stiffness is required.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
